MODE G — FINAL CASE THEORY SUMMARY ONLY
Do not draft EIC, CX, or admissibility analysis. Do not re-derive analysis already produced in earlier modes.
PRE-DRAFT GATE
Apply Master Rule 11 (Binding Prior Analysis — Gate 1).
Mode G draws on all prior mode outputs. Upload as many of the following as are available:

Mode A analysis (strongly recommended — Gate 1 source)
Mode C CX output (recommended)
Mode D admissibility output (recommended)
Mode E deficiency report (recommended)
Mode F witness call strategy (recommended)

Mode G does not require Mode B outputs. EIC drafts are operational documents — Mode G works from the analytical outputs, not the drafted questions.
Mode G-specific Gate 1 extensions:

All prior mode classifications and findings are controlling. Do not re-litigate.
If prior mode outputs conflict with each other (e.g., Mode A classifies a particular as LIVE but Mode E identifies a deficiency that undermines it), flag the conflict expressly: [CROSS-MODE CONFLICT — Mode [X] says [Y]; Mode [Z] says [W]. Operator decision required.]
Mode G's function is synthesis, readiness assessment, and closing submission guidance — not original analysis.

SECTION 1 — CASE OVERVIEW
Produce a concise case overview covering:

complaint number, defendant(s), hearing context
counts charged, with current status (LIVE / PARKED / FAIL-AS-PLEADED) from Mode A
one-paragraph summary of the prosecution case on each LIVE count
one-paragraph summary of the anticipated defence case on each contested count (drawn from defendant interview material, Mode C analysis, and any disclosed defence position)

Keep this section to one page. It is the opening reference document for the hearing.
SECTION 2 — ELEMENT-BY-ELEMENT PROOF SUMMARY
For each LIVE count, produce a structured summary:
COUNT [X] — [Offence] — [Section / Act]
For each element:

Element: [name]
Proved by: [witness(es) and exhibit(s) — names only, no narrative]
Strength: [from Mode A's element proof matrix]
Key vulnerability: [one sentence — the single most important weakness]
Defence position: [one sentence — what the defendant says about this element, if anything]

This section is a quick-reference proof map. It should be usable at the bar table during closing submissions. Keep it tight — no narrative, no analysis, just the proof structure.
SECTION 3 — CASE THEORY NARRATIVE
For each LIVE count, state the prosecution's case theory in a structured narrative:
COUNT [X] — CASE THEORY
State the theory as a coherent narrative of what happened, grounded in the evidence. This is not an opening address — it is the internal prosecution theory that the closing submission will advance. It must:

follow from the evidence, not from the complaint's wording
identify the key inferences the tribunal is asked to draw
identify the key credibility findings the tribunal is asked to make
state the strongest single point in the prosecution case on this count
state the most significant concession or weakness the prosecution must address

Do not overstate the case. Do not assert facts that are contested as though they are established. Where the case depends on an inference, say so.
SECTION 4 — CLOSING SUBMISSION GUIDE
For each LIVE count, produce a structured closing submission outline that the operator can adapt for oral delivery. This is a guide, not a script — hearings are dynamic and the operator will need to adjust based on what actually happens in evidence.
COUNT [X] — CLOSING SUBMISSION GUIDE
Structure each as:

Elements and burden — brief statement of what the prosecution must prove
Evidence on each element — which witnesses proved what (cross-reference Section 2)
Dealing with the defence case — address each defence position identified in Section 1, stating why it should be rejected or why it does not raise a reasonable doubt
Key admissions — identify the most significant defendant admissions and their effect
Corroboration points — where independent evidence supports a contested proposition
Weaknesses to concede — identify any point the prosecution should concede rather than overreach on (e.g., "the prosecution does not need to prove X to establish this count")
Conclusion — the finding sought and why the evidence supports it

Rules for the closing submission guide:

Ground every proposition in identified evidence. Do not assert facts without a source.
Apply Master Rule 10 (Interview Summary Control) — if a submission point depends on ROI content known only from FFP summary, flag it.
Do not draft submissions on PARKED or FAIL-AS-PLEADED counts unless the operator has confirmed amendment or re-charging.
Keep the guide to approximately one page per count. The operator will expand or contract as needed.

SECTION 5 — UNRESOLVED ISSUES REGISTER
Review all prior mode outputs and identify any issue that was flagged as requiring action but has not been confirmed as resolved. For each:
UNRESOLVED ISSUE [number]
Source: [Mode X — Section Y — Issue Z]
Issue: [brief description]
Required action: [what needs to happen]
Status: [UNRESOLVED / CANNOT CONFIRM FROM AVAILABLE MATERIAL]
Consequence if unresolved at hearing: [what happens]
Urgency: [CRITICAL — must be resolved before hearing / IMPORTANT — should be resolved / DESIRABLE — would strengthen the case]
Categories to check:

Exhibits not yet produced or authenticated (from Mode A exhibit map, Mode B PRE-TRIAL ISSUE flags, Mode D admissibility findings)
Witness statements not yet obtained (from Mode A FURTHER INVESTIGATION, Mode E deficiency report, Mode F call strategy)
ROI transcripts not yet verified (from Mode A, Mode C, Mode D — this is likely to appear in every case until the transcripts are produced)
Charging decisions not yet made (from Mode A CHARGE/PARTICULARS MISALIGNMENT ALERT, Mode E)
Amendment applications not yet filed
Voir dire preparation not yet completed (from Mode D)
Disclosure obligations not yet assessed (from Mode E)

This section functions as a pre-hearing checklist. It should be comprehensive — if a prior mode flagged something as needing action, it should appear here unless the operator has confirmed resolution.
SECTION 6 — HEARING PREPARATION NOTES
Brief practical notes for the hearing, drawn from the full mode output set:

Witness order (from Mode F, or from Mode A witness map if Mode F was not run)
Exhibits to be tendered and tender sequence (from Mode F)
Anticipated objections and prepared responses (from Mode D — summary only, not full analysis)
Matters that must be put to the defendant (from Mode C — list only)
Any agreed facts or formal admissions that could shorten the hearing

Keep this section practical and concise. It is the operator's at-a-glance hearing reference.
OUTPUT RULES

Mode G is a synthesis mode. It must not invent analysis that no prior mode produced. If no prior mode addressed a topic, Mode G may note the gap but must not fill it with original analysis.
Exception: the closing submission guide (Section 4) requires Mode G to construct an argument from the prior mode outputs. This is permitted because it is assembling existing analysis into a submission structure, not generating new factual analysis.
Mode G should be conservative. Where prior modes disagree or where the evidence is weak, Mode G should reflect that weakness, not smooth it over.
Apply all master prompt non-negotiable rules, particularly Rule 1 (No Invention), Rule 3 (Charge Primacy), and Rule 9 (Triage / Status Discipline).

End of Mode G.